If a model uses a compression and the decoder isn't set, GLTFLoader throws a clear error. Add the decoder and reload.

A loaded glTF is a normal THREE.Group. To wire shadows, swap materials, find named nodes:
gltf.scene.traverse((c) => {
if (c.isMesh) {
c.castShadow = true;
c.receiveShadow = true;
// optionally override material
if (c.name === 'glass_panel') {
c.material = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({
transmission: 1, roughness: 0, thickness: 0.5
});
}
}
if (c.isLight) {
c.castShadow = true;
}
});
Common finders:
const head = gltf.scene.getObjectByName('head');
const cams = gltf.cameras;
const clips = gltf.animations;
Trick: name your meshes and bones in Blender / Maya. Renaming after export is painful; well-named scene graph is gold.

If you replace a material with a fresh one, you typically want to preserve maps + colour space:
gltf.scene.traverse((c) => {
if (c.isMesh && c.material.isMeshStandardMaterial) {
const old = c.material;
c.material = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({
map: old.map,
normalMap: old.normalMap,
roughnessMap: old.roughnessMap,
metalnessMap: old.metalnessMap,
aoMap: old.aoMap,
color: old.color,
// add Physical features
clearcoat: 1.0,
clearcoatRoughness: 0.1,
});
old.dispose();
}
});
Wholesale c.material = new MeshStandardMaterial() without preserving textures gives plain-coloured meshes — common surprise.

async function loadModel(url) {
const { GLTFLoader } = await import('three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js');
const { DRACOLoader } = await import('three/addons/loaders/DRACOLoader.js');
const loader = new GLTFLoader().setDRACOLoader(
new DRACOLoader().setDecoderPath('/draco/')
);
return loader.loadAsync(url);
}
The decoders aren't in your initial JS bundle — they download only when the first model loads.
